<section class="bt-hero"><div class="bt-w bt-hero-g"><div class="bt-hero-t"><p class="bt-tag">Snacks &middot; Lunchboxes &middot; Party bites</p><h1>Small bites, properly planned.</h1>
<p class="bt-lead">Bitesly is an independent guide to the food that happens between meals. Work out snack portions for the week, pack a lunchbox that comes home empty and count the bites for a crowd, all with three free planners.</p>
<div class="bt-btns"><a class="btn" href="#planners">Open the planners</a><a class="btn bt-ghost" href="snack-prep-guide.html">Read the prep guide</a></div></div>
<figure class="bt-hero-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1771626717980-0049820ed4e1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&h=760&q=75" alt="assorted snacks and dried fruits on a wooden board" width="900" height="760" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>A board like this takes ten minutes and feeds four.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="bt-rules" aria-label="House rules"><div class="bt-w bt-rules-g"><div><b>30 g</b><span>One cupped handful of nuts, and a fair portion for an adult</span></div><div><b>2 hrs</b><span>The longest perishable food should sit out of the fridge</span></div><div><b>5 parts</b><span>Main, protein, vegetable, fruit and one extra make a full lunchbox</span></div></div></section>

<section class="bt-sec"><div class="bt-w"><p class="bt-tag">What we cover</p><h2>Three kinds of bite, three sets of rules</h2><div class="bt-kinds">
<article class="bt-kind"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1781773338966-373f0b949645?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="bowls of almonds, pistachios and dried apricots" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><h3>Everyday snacks</h3><p>The four o&rsquo;clock gap is where most people reach for whatever is nearest. A snack that pairs fibre with protein holds you until dinner, and it takes less effort than you would think once the fridge is set up for it.</p><a href="snack-prep-guide.html">How to prep a week of snacks</a></div></article>
<article class="bt-kind"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1784979472083-dd3c4c109345?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="lunchbox filled with a sandwich and cucumber slices" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><h3>Lunchboxes</h3><p>A good lunchbox is five small things, not one big one. Keep wet away from dry, cold next to the ice pack, and pack it the night before so the morning is only a matter of closing the lid.</p><a href="lunchbox-packing-guide.html">The five-part packing method</a></div></article>
<article class="bt-kind"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1774921676955-b54c02fe4fb0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="four small appetizers arranged on a tray" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><h3>Party bites</h3><p>Hosts nearly always make too many kinds and too few of each. Five well-chosen bites, in the right numbers, look more generous than nine half-empty plates and leave you free to talk to your guests.</p><a href="#planners">Count the bites you need</a></div></article>
</div></div></section>

