<label for="pb-m">The bites are&hellip;<select id="pb-m"><option value="before">A starter before a meal</option><option value="instead" selected>The whole meal</option><option value="after">Something sweet afterwards</option></select></label></div><div class="bt-out" id="pb-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="bt-sec"><div class="bt-w"><p class="bt-tag">No-recipe ideas</p><h2>A school week of snacks</h2><p class="bt-sub">Five pairings that need a knife and a chopping board, nothing more.</p><div class="bt-week">
<div><span>Mon</span><h3>Apple + peanut butter</h3><p>Slice the apple, squeeze over a little lemon and pack the nut butter in a small pot for dipping.</p></div>
<div><span>Tue</span><h3>Hummus + carrot sticks</h3><p>Cut the carrots on Sunday and keep them in water. They stay crisp until Friday.</p></div>
<div><span>Wed</span><h3>Yogurt + berries + oats</h3><p>Layer in a jar the night before. The oats soften and the whole thing eats like a pudding.</p></div>
<div><span>Thu</span><h3>Cheese + wholegrain crackers</h3><p>Cube the cheese rather than slicing it. Cubes survive a bag far better than slices.</p></div>
<div><span>Fri</span><h3>Popcorn + almonds</h3><p>Pop plain kernels in a covered pan and mix with a handful of nuts. Salt lightly while warm.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="bt-sec bt-dark"><div class="bt-w"><p class="bt-tag">Two long reads</p><h2>Guides worth half an hour</h2>
<a class="bt-row" href="snack-prep-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1701341404788-b85484ca379e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=720&h=460&q=75" alt="wooden table with bowls of crackers and snacks" width="720" height="460" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 01</span><h3>The Snack Prep Guide</h3><p>One hour on a Sunday, a fridge shelf of ready snacks, and a table of how long each one keeps.</p><em>Read the guide &rarr;</em></div></a>
<a class="bt-row bt-rev" href="lunchbox-packing-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1696677049263-cc38af1c7681?auto=format&fit=crop&w=720&h=460&q=75" alt="wooden box packed with different kinds of food" width="720" height="460" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 02</span><h3>The Lunchbox Packing Guide</h3><p>The five-part method, how to keep cold things cold, and portions for children, teenagers and adults.</p><em>Read the guide &rarr;</em></div></a>
</div></section>

<section class="bt-sec"><div class="bt-w"><p class="bt-tag">Stock up once</p><h2>The snack-ready kitchen</h2><div class="bt-stock">
<div><h3>In the cupboard</h3><ul><li>Rolled oats</li><li>Unsalted mixed nuts</li><li>Pumpkin and sunflower seeds</li><li>Wholegrain crackers</li><li>Popcorn kernels</li><li>Dried apricots and raisins</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>In the fridge</h3><ul><li>Hummus or bean dip</li><li>Plain yogurt</li><li>A block of firm cheese</li><li>Carrots, cucumber, peppers</li><li>Eggs for boiling</li><li>Lemons, for cut fruit</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>In the freezer</h3><ul><li>Mixed berries</li><li>Podded edamame</li><li>Wholemeal pitta</li><li>Banana slices</li><li>A batch of oat energy bites</li><li>Two spare ice packs</li></ul></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="bt-sec bt-tint"><div class="bt-w bt-n"><p class="bt-tag">Seen it all</p><h2>Five mistakes that spoil a good snack</h2><ol class="bt-miss">
<li><h3>Eating straight from the bag</h3><p>Nobody stops at a portion when the bag is open on the desk. Tip a handful into a bowl and put the bag back in the cupboard.</p></li>
<li><h3>All crunch, no protein</h3><p>Crackers on their own are gone from your system in forty minutes. Add cheese, hummus or a boiled egg and the same crackers last all afternoon.</p></li>
<li><h3>Packing wet next to dry</h3><p>Tomatoes beside a sandwich means a soggy sandwich by noon. Give anything juicy its own lidded pot or silicone cup.</p></li>
<li><h3>Forgetting the ice pack</h3><p>Yogurt, cheese, egg and chicken all need to stay cold. An insulated bag without an ice pack is only a bag.</p></li>
<li><h3>Too many kinds of party bite</h3><p>Every extra kind is another shopping list, another tray and another thing to plate. Make fewer kinds and more of each.</p></li>
</ol></div></section>

<section class="bt-sec"><div class="bt-w bt-n"><p class="bt-tag">Quick answers</p><h2>Questions we hear every week</h2><div class="bt-faq"><details open><summary>How big should a snack be?</summary><p>Small enough that you are still hungry for your next meal. For most adults that is a cupped handful of something filling, such as nuts or cheese, plus a piece of fruit or a few vegetable sticks. Children need roughly half of that.</p></details><details><summary>What makes a snack keep you full?</summary><p>A mix of fibre and protein. Fruit or vegetables bring the fibre and volume; yogurt, cheese, eggs, hummus or nuts bring the protein. A snack built from only one of the two tends to wear off within the hour.</p></details><details><summary>How do I keep a lunchbox cold until midday?</summary><p>Use an insulated bag with a frozen ice pack sitting next to anything perishable. A frozen yogurt tube or a frozen water bottle does the same job and is ready to eat or drink by lunchtime.</p></details><details><summary>How many party bites do I need per guest?</summary><p>Before a meal, plan on four to six pieces each. If the bites are the meal, plan on twelve to fifteen pieces each over a two to three hour party. The calculator above adjusts this for the length of your event.</p></details><details><summary>How far ahead can I prepare snacks?</summary><p>Most cut vegetables, boiled eggs, hummus and energy bites hold for four to five days in sealed containers in the fridge. Cut apples, avocado and anything dressed are best made on the day.</p></details></div></div></section>

